<%
  // og:image, and the two properties that describe it.
  //
  // A partial rather than a line in each of the twenty-three views that need
  // one, because the three tags have to be decided together. `locals.image` is
  // a game's own cover, served from the media bucket, and nothing here knows
  // how many pixels it is; the site's fallback is a file in public/ whose size
  // is a constant. So the width/height pair can be stated for one and not for
  // the other, and a guessed pair is worse than an absent one — a scraper that
  // believes it lays out the wrong box. The same reasoning views/head.ejs
  // gives for twitter:image.
  //
  // They also have to be *after* the og:image they describe: Open Graph reads
  // a structured property as belonging to the last root property named, so
  // these cannot live in head.ejs, which is included above this point.
  const ogImage = locals.image || siteImage;
%>
<meta property="og:image" content="<%= ogImage %>" />
<%
  // What the picture is of, for a reader who cannot see it.
  //
  // views/head.ejs has emitted twitter:image:alt on every page for a while
  // and reasons out there why: a card with an image and nothing describing it
  // is what a screen reader announces as an unlabelled attachment. The Open
  // Graph half of that pair was written in exactly one view —
  // views/games/game-detail.ejs — so a link to any of the other twenty-two
  // pages was shared to Facebook, LinkedIn, Slack and Discord with an
  // undescribed picture.
  //
  // Same value as the Twitter tag, deliberately: the page title is what the
  // image shows, whether it is a game's cover art or the site's own banner
  // with the name on it. Two tags describing one image should not disagree,
  // and this is the partial that already exists to keep them from it.
%>
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="<%= locals.title || siteTitle %>" />
<% if (!locals.image) { %>
<meta property="og:image:width" content="<%= siteImageWidth %>" />
<meta property="og:image:height" content="<%= siteImageHeight %>" />
<% } %>
